# UPSSSC JA 3284 pattern passages, set 2 (English).
EN = [
("Dhanteras", "Dhanteras is celebrated two days before Deepawali and marks the start of the five day festival of lights. On this day people worship Lord Dhanvantari, the god of health, and Goddess Lakshmi, the goddess of wealth. It is believed that buying something new on Dhanteras brings good luck to the family. Markets stay open till late at night and are full of people. Many families buy gold or silver jewellery, coins and new utensils made of steel or brass. Some people buy new vehicles, mobile phones and home appliances on this day. In the evening, a lamp is lit at the main door of the house to keep away bad luck. Shopkeepers decorate their shops with flowers and lights and offer special discounts. People should be careful while buying precious items. They should check the hallmark on gold, ask for a proper bill and avoid buying more than they need. The real meaning of Dhanteras is not only wealth in the form of money. Good health is also a great wealth, and the festival reminds us to take care of our body and mind."),
("Bhai Dooj", "Bhai Dooj is celebrated two days after Deepawali and is the last day of the festival of lights. It is a festival of love between brothers and sisters, just like Raksha Bandhan. On this day sisters invite their brothers to their home and welcome them with great joy. They put a tilak on the forehead of their brothers, do aarti and pray for their long and healthy life. Brothers give gifts to their sisters and promise to stand by them in every situation. Special food and sweets are made in every home. An old story says that Yamraj, the god of death, visited his sister Yamuna on this day. She welcomed him with love, and he gave her a blessing that any brother who visits his sister on this day will be safe from untimely death. In some regions the festival is called Bhau Beej or Bhai Phonta. Married sisters who live far away wait the whole year for this day. Bhai Dooj teaches us that the love and trust between family members are the real wealth of life."),
("Janmashtami", "Janmashtami is the festival that marks the birth of Lord Krishna. It falls in the month of Bhadrapada, usually in August or September. Lord Krishna was born at midnight in a prison in Mathura, where his parents Devaki and Vasudeva were kept by the cruel king Kansa. On this day many people keep a fast and break it only after midnight, when the birth of Krishna is celebrated. Temples and homes are decorated with flowers, lights and small cradles for baby Krishna. Children dress up as Krishna and Radha, and songs and dances about his childhood are performed. The festival is celebrated with special joy in Mathura and Vrindavan, where thousands of people gather in the temples. In Maharashtra, young men form human pyramids to break a pot of curd hung high above the street. This event is called Dahi Handi and reminds people of the playful childhood of Krishna, who loved butter. The teachings of Krishna in the Bhagavad Gita tell us to do our duty without worrying about the result. Janmashtami fills our hearts with love and devotion."),
("Navratri", "Navratri means nine nights and is one of the most important festivals of India. It is celebrated twice a year, but the Navratri that comes before Dussehra is the most famous. During these nine days, people worship the nine forms of Goddess Durga, who is the symbol of strength and courage. Many people keep a fast for all nine days and eat only fruits and simple food. Temples are decorated, and special prayers are held every morning and evening. In Gujarat, people dance the Garba and Dandiya at night in colourful dresses. In West Bengal, huge pandals are set up for Durga Puja, and beautiful idols of the goddess are worshipped. In many homes, young girls are invited on the eighth or ninth day, and they are given food and gifts as a form of the goddess. The festival ends with Dussehra, which celebrates the victory of good over evil. Navratri teaches us that every woman deserves respect and that we must fight against the evil within ourselves. It also brings people together through music, dance and faith."),
("Christmas", "Christmas is celebrated every year on the twenty fifth of December to mark the birth of Jesus Christ. It is one of the most loved festivals in the world, and people of all faiths in India enjoy it. Jesus taught people to love one another, to forgive and to help the poor. Weeks before Christmas, churches and homes are decorated with stars, bells and colourful lights. A Christmas tree is set up and decorated with small gifts, balls and shining paper. On Christmas Eve, people go to church for a special midnight prayer and sing carols. Children believe that Santa Claus comes at night and leaves gifts for them. Families and friends meet, share cakes and sweets and exchange gifts. In Goa, Kerala and the north east, Christmas is celebrated with special joy, and the markets of big cities are full of lights. Many people visit orphanages and old age homes and share food and gifts with those in need. Christmas reminds us that the greatest gift we can give is love and kindness to others."),
("Teachers' Day", "Teachers' Day is celebrated in India on the fifth of September every year. It is the birthday of Dr Sarvepalli Radhakrishnan, a great teacher and thinker who later became the second President of India. When his students wanted to celebrate his birthday, he asked them to celebrate it as Teachers' Day instead. Teachers play a very important role in our life. They give us education, which is the most precious gift, and they help us to become good human beings. A good teacher not only teaches lessons from books but also teaches us discipline, honesty and respect for others. On Teachers' Day, students thank their teachers with cards, flowers and small gifts. In many schools, senior students take the place of teachers for one day and teach the younger classes. Cultural programmes, songs and speeches are held to honour the teachers. The government also gives national awards to the best teachers of the country. We should not respect our teachers only on one day. We should follow their advice and make them proud through our hard work and good conduct."),
("Children's Day", "Children's Day is celebrated in India on the fourteenth of November every year. It is the birthday of Pandit Jawaharlal Nehru, the first Prime Minister of India. He loved children very much, and they called him Chacha Nehru with love. He believed that children are the future of the nation and that they must get good education, good health and a happy childhood. On this day schools organise games, quiz contests, drawing competitions, songs and dances. Teachers sometimes perform for the children and give them sweets and small gifts. Many programmes are held on television and radio for children. The day also reminds us of the problems that many children still face. Some children cannot go to school because they have to work to help their families. Some do not get enough food or proper care. It is the duty of the government and of every citizen to make sure that every child gets a fair chance in life. When children are healthy, educated and happy, the nation becomes strong and bright."),
("Review of a Laptop", "I bought a new laptop three months ago for my studies and online work, and here is my honest review. The laptop is light and easy to carry in a bag. Its body is made of good plastic and feels strong. The screen is clear and bright, and the colours look natural, so watching videos and reading notes is comfortable. The keyboard is the best part for me, because the keys are soft and quiet, and typing for a long time does not tire the fingers. The touchpad works smoothly. The laptop starts within a few seconds and runs common software like documents, browsers and video calls without any problem. Heavy software for video editing makes it slow and the fan becomes loud. The battery lasts for about six to seven hours with normal use, which is enough for a full day of classes. The speakers are a little weak, so I use earphones. The webcam is average. Overall, this laptop is a good choice for students and office workers who want a light machine with a good keyboard at a fair price."),
("Review of a Train Journey", "Last month I travelled from Lucknow to Delhi by a new express train, and the journey was a pleasant surprise. I booked my ticket online two weeks in advance, and the process was simple and quick. The train started on time from the station. The coach was clean, and the seats were soft and comfortable, with enough space for the legs. Each seat had a charging point and a reading light, which was very useful. The staff were polite and kept checking the cleanliness of the coach and the toilets. Hot tea and breakfast were served in the morning. The food was simple but fresh and tasty. The windows were large, so I could enjoy the view of green fields, rivers and small villages on the way. The train did not stop at many stations, so we reached Delhi almost on time. The only problem was that the air conditioning was too cold, and I had to ask for a blanket. On the whole, it was a comfortable and safe journey, and I would happily travel by this train again."),
("Review of a Hotel Stay", "During my visit to Varanasi last winter, I stayed for two nights in a small hotel near the ghats, and I want to share my experience. I booked the room online, and the hotel staff called me a day before to confirm the booking. The reception was simple but clean, and the check in took only a few minutes. The room was not very large, but it was neat, with a comfortable bed, clean sheets and a small table. Hot water was available in the bathroom all day, which was a great relief in the cold weather. From the terrace, we could see the river and the evening aarti, which was a beautiful sight. The breakfast had poha, paratha, tea and fruits, and everything was fresh. The staff were helpful and told us about the best places to visit and the right time to take a boat ride. The only problem was the noise from the street in the early morning. The price was fair for the location. I would suggest this hotel to families and students who want a clean and simple stay near the ghats."),
("Dr B R Ambedkar", "Dr Bhimrao Ramji Ambedkar was a great leader, thinker and social reformer of India. He was born in a poor family in Madhya Pradesh and faced untouchability and insults from his childhood. In spite of all these problems, he never gave up his studies. With hard work and the help of some well wishers, he studied in America and England and earned high degrees in law and economics. He believed that education is the key to freedom and dignity. He gave the message, educate, agitate and organise. He fought all his life for the rights of the poor, the workers, women and those who were treated as untouchables. After independence, he became the first Law Minister of India and the chairman of the Drafting Committee of the Constitution. He is therefore called the father of the Indian Constitution. The Constitution gives equal rights to every citizen and bans untouchability. His birthday is celebrated every year with great respect. Dr Ambedkar was given the Bharat Ratna after his death. His life teaches us that knowledge and courage can change society."),
("Chandrashekhar Azad", "Chandrashekhar Azad was one of the bravest revolutionaries of the freedom struggle. He was born in a village in Madhya Pradesh. As a young boy, he joined the Non Cooperation Movement and was arrested. When the judge asked his name, he said that his name was Azad, his father's name was Swatantrata and his home was the jail. From that day he came to be known as Azad. After the movement was called off, he joined a group of young revolutionaries who believed in armed struggle against the British. He became one of the leaders of the Hindustan Socialist Republican Association along with Bhagat Singh and his friends. He took part in many brave actions against British rule. Azad had promised that he would never be caught alive by the British. In Alfred Park in Prayagraj, he was surrounded by the police. He fought alone with great courage, and when he had only one bullet left, he used it on himself. The park is now called Chandrashekhar Azad Park. His courage and love for the nation still inspire the youth of India."),
("Lal Bahadur Shastri", "Lal Bahadur Shastri was the second Prime Minister of India and a man of great honesty and simple living. He was born in Mughalsarai in Uttar Pradesh in a poor family. As a child, he had to cross the Ganga by swimming to reach his school. He joined the freedom struggle at a young age and went to jail many times. After independence, he served as a minister in many important departments. When a train accident happened while he was the Railway Minister, he resigned, taking moral responsibility, which shows his honesty. He became Prime Minister after the death of Pandit Nehru. During his time, India fought a war with Pakistan, and the country also faced a shortage of food. He gave the famous slogan Jai Jawan Jai Kisan to honour the soldiers who protect the borders and the farmers who feed the nation. He asked people to skip one meal a week to save food. He died in Tashkent after signing a peace agreement. He was given the Bharat Ratna after his death. His life teaches us honesty, simplicity and selfless service."),
("Dr A P J Abdul Kalam", "Dr A P J Abdul Kalam is known as the Missile Man of India and the People's President. He was born in a poor family in Rameswaram in Tamil Nadu. As a boy, he sold newspapers to help his family, but he never stopped studying. He studied aerospace engineering and joined the space and defence research work of the country. He played a key role in the development of India's satellite launch vehicle and its missile programme. He was also closely linked with the nuclear tests at Pokhran. He became the eleventh President of India and was loved by people of all ages, especially students. He travelled across the country and spoke to lakhs of young people about dreams, hard work and education. He said that dreams are not those we see in sleep, but those that do not let us sleep. He wrote many books, including Wings of Fire, which tells the story of his life. He died while giving a lecture to students in Shillong. He was given the Bharat Ratna. His life shows that a person from a simple family can reach great heights through hard work."),
("International Yoga Day", "International Yoga Day is celebrated every year on the twenty first of June, which is the longest day of the year in the northern half of the world. The idea of celebrating this day was proposed by India at the United Nations, and it was accepted with the support of a large number of countries. Yoga is an ancient Indian practice that joins the body, mind and breath. It includes simple exercises called asanas, breathing exercises called pranayama and meditation. Regular practice of yoga makes the body flexible and strong, keeps the mind calm and helps in controlling stress, weight and many diseases. On Yoga Day, large events are held in parks, schools, offices and public places where thousands of people do yoga together. Leaders, film stars and sportspersons also take part and encourage others. Yoga does not need costly equipment. A small mat and a few minutes every morning are enough to begin. In today's busy life, where people sit for long hours in front of screens, yoga can bring balance and good health. We should make yoga a part of our daily life."),
]
